[37.378412, "o", "sed -n '1,220p' README.md 2>/dev/null || true\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[37.381596, "o", "# flowctl\r\n\r\n`flowctl` is an in-house command-line tool the data platform team is building\r\nto wire together small ETL pipelines. Analysts drop a YAML pipeline file onto\r\ndisk, point `flowctl run` at it, and get a transformed record stream out the\r\nother side without needing to write bespoke glue every time.\r\n\r\nThe project lives in `/app/` and is built with Go (module path\r\n"]
[37.381699, "o", "`example.com/flowctl`). The binary is produced with `go build -o flowctl\r\n./cmd/flowctl`.\r\n\r\n## Scope\r\n\r\nThe first milestone of `flowctl` focuses on the integration wiring between:\r\n\r\n- a single structured **input source** (a file on disk),\r\n- an ordered list of **stages** that reshape each record, and\r\n- a single **output sink** (also a file on disk, or stdout).\r\n\r\nEverything runs in-process; there is no daemon, no network, no database.\r\n\r\n## Layout\r\n\r\n```\r\n/app/\r\n├── go.mod\r\n├── cmd/flowctl/        # CLI entry point\r\n├── internal/...        # pipeline building blocks\r\n└── examples/           # sample pipelines the team uses for smoke tests\r\n```\r\n\r\n"]
[37.381787, "o", "`examples/` ships a couple of tiny fixtures that predate this milestone. They\r\nare only there so new hires can eyeball the file formats; the production\r\npipelines use larger files mounted in at runtime.\r\n\r\n## Running\r\n"]
[37.381904, "o", "\r\n```\r\nflowctl run --config path/to/pipeline.yaml\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe command reads `--config`, opens the input file declared inside, runs each\r\nrecord through the declared stages, and writes the survivors to the declared\r\noutput. A one-line summary is printed to stderr so it can be captured by the\r\nsurrounding job scheduler.\r\n\r\n## Conventions\r\n\r\n- All text is UTF-8.\r\n- Record field names are case-sensitive.\r\n- The tool must be deterministic: the same inputs always produce the same\r\n  bytes on the output, in the same order.\r\n- Empty output is legal (`0` records emitted is a successful run).\r\n"]
[37.382354, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[37.382381, "o", "root@n30009:/app# "]
[44.357567, "i", "printf '%s\\n' '--- examples/pipeline.yaml ---'; cat examples/pipeline.yaml; printf '%s\\n' '--- examples/orders.csv ---'; cat examples/orders.csv\n"]
[44.358524, "o", "printf '%s\\n' '--- examples/pipeline.yaml ---'; cat examples/pipeline.yaml; printf '%s\\n' '--- examples/orders.csv ---'; cat examples/orders.csv\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[44.358677, "o", "--- examples/pipeline.yaml ---\r\n"]
[44.361176, "o", "# Smoke pipeline used by new hires to verify their local build. Do not rely on\r\n# it in tests: the real pipelines live elsewhere and are passed in at runtime.\r\ninput:\r\n  path: examples/orders.csv\r\n  format: csv\r\nstages: []\r\noutput:\r\n  path: examples/orders.out.jsonl\r\n  format: jsonl\r\n"]
[44.361514, "o", "--- examples/orders.csv ---\r\n"]
